SBCSD patrol operations are organized by station, each covering a distinct area of the county. Deputies are assigned to a station during onboarding and may request a transfer through their chain of command.

<AccordionGroup>
  <Accordion title="Rancho Cucamonga Station" icon="building-2">
    Covers the west end of the county — dense urban and suburban patrol with heavy retail and commercial districts.

    **What to expect**

    - High call volume and short response times
    - Frequent traffic enforcement along the I-15 and Foothill corridors
    - Backup is usually close by
    - Regular coordination with CHP on freeway incidents

    Best suited to deputies who want steady, fast-paced patrol work.
  </Accordion>

  <Accordion title="Victorville Station" icon="mountain">
    Covers the High Desert, north of the Cajon Pass — a much larger patrol area mixing urban centers with open desert highway.

    **What to expect**

    - Longer response times and greater distances between calls
    - Significant coverage along the I-15 north corridor
    - More self-reliance; backup may be several minutes out
    - Lower call volume, but calls tend to run longer

    Best suited to deputies comfortable handling calls independently.
  </Accordion>
</AccordionGroup>

## Choosing a Station

The two stations offer genuinely different patrol experiences. Rancho Cucamonga is high-tempo with support close at hand; Victorville is quieter but demands more independence. Neither is considered a more senior assignment than the other.

<Note>
  Station assignment does not affect rank or division eligibility. Deputies in specialized divisions may be called countywide regardless of home station.
</Note>
